Conformity Statement:
Met by component: Unique human user identification and authentication are strictly and role-basely enforced across all physical and network interfaces on the Debian 12 platform (AFE-E630).
●Identity Uniqueness: The operating system strictly blocks credential sharing by enforcing unique User IDs (UIDs) and Group IDs (GIDs) centrally mapped within /etc/passwd and /etc/group.
●Credential Uniqueness Layer: To prevent credential reuse, the Linux PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) stack incorporates pam_pwhistory.so within the /etc/pam.d/common-password control sequence. This configuration forces a strict memory history depth that forbids users from recycling any of their last 5 previously utilized passwords, maintaining definitive individual accountability.
●Interface Enforcement: This cryptographic uniqueness mechanism covers all local graphical (LXDM via HDMI), local serial (UART Console), and remote network (OpenSSH 9.2p1) session establishment windows, aligning with the role boundaries defined under CR 2.1.
